  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSurface high pressure will continue to largely influence the \nforecast through the first half of this week. Low pressure tracking \neast across Ontario into Quebec will drag a cold front across the \nregion on Tuesday. Not anticipating any rain chances with this cold \nfront. Lows tonight will fall into the upper 30s to lower 40s for \nmost, low to mid 30s for inland locations in Northeast Ohio and \nNorthwest Pennsylvania. Mild tomorrow with highs in the upper 50s to \nlower 60s across the region. Cooler again on Tuesday night with lows \nin the low to mid 30s, upper 30s closer to the lakeshore. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nDry and mild conditions will come to an end Wednesday into Thursday \nas a large upper trough and embedded shortwaves move into the \neastern CONUS. Precipitation chances increase Wednesday afternoon \nonward as surface low pressure pushes eastward into the Lower Ohio \nValley from the Central Plains. Precipitation type will initially \nbegin as rain Wednesday night into Thursday. With the introduction \nof cooler air aloft (850mb temperatures dipping between -4 and -7C \nby Thursday night), there is the potential for some wet snow flakes \nto mix in with rain Thursday night into Friday. \n\nThe low will exit to the east and introduce steady northerly flow \ncoupled with plenty of low level moisture and additional shortwaves \naloft. This will bring a prolonged period of lake effect rain/snow \nshowers downwind of the lakes Friday into the weekend. Overall, snow \naccumulations look minimal and confined to higher elevation \nlocations across Northwest Pennsylvania. An area of high pressure \nwill move overhead from the west, but lake effect showers will \nlikely continue into early next week as low level moisture will be \nslow to decrease.",
